{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310591,"nonce":"0F9ivRYX2Sxg3bai7yw3nq4kQic=","title":"BytesBuffer compiles ALONE but fails E0803 on b.length when compiled inside Lambda's import graph. Candidate owners are bogus (`Type`), and ...","message":"**`Lambda` cannot be used at all.** Any program touching it fails:\n\n```\nerror[IMPORT]: imported module `BytesBuffer` failed to compile (4 error(s))\n  E0803: ambiguous field access: `length` exists on multiple classes\n         (Type (slot 1), \u003cunknown class\u003e (slot 3)) and the receiver's class\n         could not be resolved.\n```\n\nRepro: `class M { static function main() { Sys.println(Lambda.count([1,2,3])); } }`\n12 report rows mention E0803/E0801. Blocks Lambda, haxe.io.BytesBuffer,\nhaxe.io.Output.\n\n\n`import haxe.io.BytesBuffer; ... bb.length` **compiles and runs fine on its\nown.** The SAME source fails only when BytesBuffer is compiled as part of\nLambda's import graph. Same file, same target, different MODULE CONTEXT.\n\n`UInt` alone is fine, so this is not the preprocessor fix (2e9191f7) that\nunmasked it.\n\n\nThe source is `haxe/io/BytesBuffer.hx:72`, `return b.length;` with\n`var b:Array\u003cInt\u003e` on our target. So the receiver IS `Array\u003cInt\u003e`.\n\n`resolve_field_index_candidates` (ir/mir/resolve/lookups.rs:1403) collects\ncandidates by scanning the WHOLE `field_index_map` for any symbol whose name\nstring equals `length` -- deliberately, to survive cross-context\nInternedString mismatches. In Lambda's graph that yields two candidates whose\nowners are `Type` and `\u003cunknown class\u003e`, and **neither is Array**: the real\nArray candidate is missing while two unrelated ones match by name. Disambiguation\nthen needs `recv_bare`, which is None, so it reports ambiguity.\n\nOwner names come from `field_class_names`, a **`BTreeMap\u003cSymbolId, String\u003e`**\nassigned wholesale from another module's map at ir/mir/mod.rs:1154\n(`context.field_class_names = external_field_class_names`). SymbolIds are\nPER-CONTEXT -- see [[bugs-stdtypes-type-addition-breaks-nue]], whose rule is\nthat ONLY FQNs may cross contexts. A SymbolId-keyed map carried across the\nboundary is how an owner ends up reading `Type`.\n\n\nThe fix is to key the cross-module field maps by FQN rather than SymbolId, or\nto resolve `Array\u003cInt\u003e.length` through Array's own field table instead of a\nname scan over every field in the program. Both touch shared resolution state\nin a compiler with a recorded history of silent miscompiles from exactly that\narea. Do it with the corpus + haxe suite as gates, and check that BytesBuffer\ncompiles BOTH alone and inside Lambda's graph.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_e0803_length_ambiguous_only_in_lambda_graph` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}